# ADR-0003 — Pluggable Transcription Engine

Status: Accepted
Date: 2026-06-05

## Context

Sapiens Note turns recordings into text. Speech recognition needs more compute
than a small device has, so it runs off-device (see ADR-0004) — in the local web
dashboard (`sapiens-web`). The question: hard-wire one transcription backend, or
make it swappable between a local model and a cloud API?

## Decision

Use a **single small interface** for transcription with **swappable
implementations**, chosen at runtime:

```cpp
struct ITranscriber {
    TranscriptionResult transcribe(const std::string& wav_path);
};
```

| Engine | Selector | Needs |
|---|---|---|
| **whisper.cpp** (local, offline) | `--engine whisper` *(default)* | a ggml model (`SAPIENS_WHISPER_MODEL`) |
| **OpenAI Whisper API** (cloud) | `--engine openai` | `OPENAI_API_KEY` |
| **fake** (demo/tests) | `--engine fake` | nothing — canned text |

Each engine is a thin adapter producing the same `TranscriptionResult`. The
result is written back through the core (`NoteService::apply_transcription`), so
it appears in the simulators immediately.

## Consequences

Positive:
- run fully offline (whisper.cpp) or fully cloud (OpenAI), your choice
- swap or upgrade the engine without touching the rest of the app
- the `fake` engine makes the round-trip testable with no model or key

Negative:
- two real engines to support
- transcription quality is bounded by the chosen model, not the app
